`fetch`'s `cache` option lets you override the default HTTP caching behavior per request, and `AbortController` gives you a real timeout, which `fetch` doesn't provide natively:

```javascript
async function getFreshData(url) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 5000);

  try {
    const res = await fetch(url, {
      cache: 'no-store',           // bypass the HTTP cache entirely
      signal: controller.signal,
    });
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.name === 'AbortError') throw new Error('Request timed out');
    throw err;
  } finally {
    clearTimeout(timeoutId);
  }
}
```

`cache: 'reload'` forces a network fetch but still updates the cache; `cache: 'no-store'` never touches the cache at all, useful for genuinely one-off, never-repeat requests like a logout call.
